# Agent worktrees — work in your own worktree, commit your own work

Standing policy (Lukas, 2026-08-14): each Cursor agent runs in its own git
worktree and commits its finished work to the repo from there.

## When your session is in a worktree

You're in a worktree if the path is under `~/.cursor/worktrees/` or
`git rev-parse --git-common-dir` is not `.git`.

- You have **standing permission to commit your own completed work** on the
  worktree's branch — no need to ask first. Commit when a task is done (or
  at coherent milestones in a long task), not after every edit.
- Stage only the files YOU changed for the task (`git add <paths>`; never
  `git add -A` / `git commit -a` — pre-existing dirt is not yours).
- Message style matches `git log`: `<area>: <what and why>`, e.g.
  `dynrep: quality/retention eval instrumentation`.
- Do **not** push, merge into the main branch, or delete branches unless the
  user asks. Merging back is the user's call (`/apply-worktree`, or a PR when
  requested).

## Worktree environment (set up automatically)

`.cursor/worktrees.json` runs `.cursor/setup-worktree-unix.sh` in each new
worktree. It writes an `.envrc` that activates the MAIN checkout's `.venv`
(never create a per-worktree venv), symlinks
`hexapod_walker/prototype_sts3215/rl_move/sim/policies` to the main
checkout's checkpoint cache (shared — never delete zips from a worktree),
and copies `.mcp.json`. If imports fail in a fresh worktree, run that script
by hand with `ROOT_WORKTREE_PATH=/Users/lukas/weird_objects`.

## When your session is in the main checkout

The default rules stand: only commit when the user asks in the current
session. Prefer starting substantive work in a worktree (`/worktree`).
